Learning GitHub Actions – Day 14: Path Filters
Continuing Phase 2: Workflow Configuration in my GitHub Actions learning journey!

📌 What are Path Filters?
Path filters in GitHub Actions allow us to control when a workflow runs based on the files or directories that have changed in a repository.
Instead of triggering a workflow for every code change, we can configure it to run only when specific files are modified.
This helps reduce unnecessary workflow executions and makes CI/CD pipelines more efficient.

🛠️ Practical Example:

name: Path Filter Demo

  on:
   push:
     paths:
       - 'src/**'
       - 'Dockerfile'

  jobs:
   build:
     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
     steps:
       - name: Display Message
         run: echo "Changes detected in application files!"

🔍 How it works:
paths: Defines which file paths should trigger the workflow.
src/**: Triggers the workflow when files inside the src directory or its subdirectories change.
Dockerfile: Triggers the workflow when the root-level Dockerfile changes.
Changes to unrelated files, such as README.md, will not trigger this workflow.

💡 Key Takeaway:
Path filters help run workflows only when relevant files change, saving CI/CD execution time and resources.
